{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Abraham came</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 23:2): from Beer-sheba. And this is not to say that he was standing there, from what is written (Genesis 22:19), \"And Abraham dwelt in Beer-sheba,\" for how could Sarah have been in Hebron? Rather, the intent is to say that he went there during the day for his needs, and heard of Sarah's death, and came from there to mourn for her and to weep for her.\n\nAnd the language of our Sages (Bereshit Rabbah 58:5) is: \"from Mount Moriah he came.\" And so it is according to the midrash that the Rav wrote (Rashi here), for she heard of the Akedah and her soul departed. And what appears regarding the Akedah is that the command concerning it was in Beer-sheba, for there he dwelt and there he returned, for thus it is written at the beginning (Genesis 21:33), \"And he planted an eshel in Beer-sheba and called there on the name of YHWH, El Olam,\" and it says (ibid.), \"And Abraham sojourned in the land of the Philistines many days,\" and this is his sojourning in Beer-sheba, which is in the land of the Philistines. And there he was commanded concerning the Akedah, and therefore he tarried on the road three days, for the land of the Philistines is far from Jerusalem — though Hebron is in the hill country of Judah, as is written (Joshua 20:7), and it is close to Jerusalem. And on his return from the Akedah he came back to Beer-sheba, as is stated (Genesis 22:19), \"And Abraham returned to his young men, and they rose and went together to Beer-sheba, and Abraham dwelt in Beer-sheba\" — to indicate that he delayed there and dwelt in it for years. If so, Sarah did not die at that time, for Abraham would not have been dwelling in Beer-sheba while Sarah dwelt in Hebron.\n\nSo too it appears that Isaac was born in Beer-sheba, for it is written first (Genesis 20:1), \"And Abraham journeyed from there toward the land of the Negev, and dwelt between Kadesh and Shur, and sojourned in Gerar,\" and Abimelech said to him, \"Behold, my land is before you; where it is good in your eyes, dwell\" (v. 15), and he settled in that land, in Beer-sheba, for thus it is written (Genesis 21:22), \"And it came to pass at that time that Abimelech and Phicol the captain of his host spoke to Abraham, saying,\" and it is not written that they came to him from Gerar, and they made the covenant there in Beer-sheba. So too you will see that Hagar, when they sent her out from Abraham's house on the day Isaac was weaned, went into the wilderness of Beer-sheba (Genesis 21:14), for there they were dwelling.\n\nBut after many days he journeyed from the land of the Philistines and came to Hebron, and there the righteous woman died.\n\nBut according to the midrash, we must say that Abraham and Sarah at the time of the Akedah were dwelling in Hebron, and there he was commanded. And what it says, \"On the third day Abraham lifted up his eyes\" (Genesis 22:4) — for the mountain desired by Elohim was not revealed to him until the third day, and during the first two days he was walking around the vicinity of Jerusalem, and it was not the will to show it to him. And Abraham, after the Akedah, did not return to his place in Hebron, but first went to Beer-sheba, the place of his eshel, to give thanks for his miracle, and there he heard of Sarah's death and came. And he who said \"from Mount Moriah he came\" and he who said \"from Beer-sheba he came\" — it is all one reasoning. And according to this, what the verse says, \"And Abraham dwelt in Beer-sheba\" (Genesis 22:19), its meaning will be that on his return from the Akedah he returned to Beer-sheba, and from there he went to bury Sarah, and immediately after the burial he returned to Beer-sheba and settled there for years. And Scripture completed the matter of Beer-sheba as one, and afterward recounted the burial. And there in Beer-sheba Isaac took Rebecca as wife, as it says, \"And he was dwelling in the land of the Negev\" (Genesis 24:62). And this is the opinion of all the commentators (Rashi, Ibn Ezra, and Radak), that Abraham was in another place and came from there.\n\nAnd in my opinion, Sarah had a tent standing there with her maidservants before her, as is written (Genesis 31:33), \"in Jacob's tent, and in Leah's tent, and in the tent of the two maidservants.\" And Sarah died in her own tent, and Abraham entered the tent with the assembly of his kin to mourn her. Or else the expression \"and he came\" may mean that Abraham roused himself for this eulogy and began to perform it, for anyone who rouses himself and begins a task is called \"coming to it,\" and this is a familiar expression in the words of the Sages, as we learned in Tamid (4:3): \"he came to the pile and placed on it two pieces of wood from here; he came to the left flank; he came to the tail,\" and their saying (Bekhorot 20a), \"I did not come to this measure,\" and so too in the verse (Exodus 22:14), \"he comes with his hire\" — he came to that labor and performed it for the sake of his hire. And it is not acceptable in my eyes that he came from another city to Hebron, for if that were so, he would have mentioned that place, and the verse would have specified, \"And Abraham heard, and he came from such-and-such a place.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The parenthetical attributions to Rashi, Ibn Ezra, and Radak in the manuscript appear to be inserted glosses identifying the commentators Ramban refers to collectively as 'all the commentators'; translated as they appear in the source text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:33:45.524399086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}